{"parentUuid":"bf7083a6-a050-4e11-a798-721fc36df9ea","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae3c21075cb388b14","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01SgyD7M13QmCto2uSG2jH6A","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ED1cBzmPwbpMxRDaNy13ye","name":"WebSearch","input":{"query":"SAT subsumption cache infeasibility certificate subset dominance pruning"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8005,"cache_read_input_tokens":13113,"output_tokens":247,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8005},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":6,"output_tokens":247,"cache_read_input_tokens":13113,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8005,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":8005,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}},"requestId":"req_011CaordUTu533LcxgB16uqt","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"19e8c517-a481-4330-9c8a-865a85d82ce3","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:17.484Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"9adca94a-d6cd-4715-88eb-d9433d8c204f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"ae3c21075cb388b14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Tv5WKt2cfvEXbhVNq1L3Dg","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"logic-based Benders decomposition subproblem caching memoization no-good cuts\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition - John Hooker\",\"url\":\"https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/encyLBBD.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Speeding Up Logic-Based Benders Decomposition by Strengthening Cuts with Graph Neural Networks | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-53969-5_3\"},{\"title\":\"Combining optimisation and simulation using logic-based Benders decomposition - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S037722172300591X\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition for Large-Scale Optimization | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-030-22788-3_1\"},{\"title\":\"[1910.11944] Logic-based Benders decomposition for large-scale optimization\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1910.11944\"},{\"title\":\"Computational Evaluation of Cut-Strengthening Techniques in Logic-Based Benders’ Decomposition | Operations Research Forum | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s43069-023-00242-3\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-Based Benders Decomposition\",\"url\":\"https://www.emergentmind.com/topics/logic-based-benders-decomposition-lbbd-4e47b3ae-690a-44a4-9d80-a191b385b4e6\"},{\"title\":\"Stochastic Planning and Scheduling with Logic-Based Benders Decomposition | INFORMS Journal on Computing\",\"url\":\"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/ijoc.2022.1184\"},{\"title\":\"ULD Build-Up Scheduling with Logic-Based Benders Decomposition | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-60597-0_17\"},{\"title\":\"Logic-based Benders Decomposition for Large-scale Optimization J. N. Hooker\",\"url\":\"https://johnhooker.tepper.cmu.edu/LBBDbookChapter.pdf\"}]\n\nI've performed the web search on your query. The results provide substantial information about logic-based Benders decomposition and its related concepts.\n\n## Key Findings:\n\n**Core Concept:**\nLogic-based Benders decomposition (LBBD) is a substantial generalization of classical Benders decomposition that allows the subproblem to be any optimization problem rather than specifically a linear or nonlinear programming problem.\n\n**No-Good Cuts:**\nThe simplest Benders cuts are known as no-good cuts which originate with the work of Balas and Jeroslow on unit hypercubes. Logical deductions from solving a constraint program can be used to generate cuts in the form of no-good inequalities for addition to a mathematical optimisation problem, though they are typically weak—causing slow convergence of the LBBD scheme.\n\n**Subproblem Solutions and Cuts:**\nLBBD derives a cut from the inference dual of the subproblem, which is the problem of finding the tightest bound on the optimal value that can be logically deduced from the constraints, with the dual solution being a proof of this bound.\n\n**Cut Strengthening:**\nIt is critical for computational performance that cuts are strengthened, but the strengthening of cuts comes at the cost of solving additional subproblems. Cut-strengthening techniques that generate irreducible cuts outperform the greedy algorithm and the use of no cut strengthening.\n\nThe search results don't specifically address caching or memoization techniques for LBBD subproblems, though they discuss various approaches to managing and optimizing the cut generation process.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"4f4adca0-e534-4e22-83f9-422fff3ab8f4","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:23.377Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"9adca94a-d6cd-4715-88eb-d9433d8c204f","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"bf7083a6-a050-4e11-a798-721fc36df9ea","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"ae3c21075cb388b14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019ee1a9LmWiYobYGesBXAyy","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT OR-Tools incremental solving model reuse learned clauses\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"GitHub - d-krupke/cpsat-primer: The CP-SAT Primer: Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/d-krupke/cpsat-primer\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT Solver | OR-Tools | Google for Developers\",\"url\":\"https://developers.google.com/optimization/cp/cp_solver\"},{\"title\":\"OR-Tools Laurent Perron (lperron@google.com)\",\"url\":\"https://egon.cheme.cmu.edu/ewo/docs/CP-SAT%20and%20OR-Tools.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"How does it work? - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/07_under_the_hood.html\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding CP-SAT: Constraint Programming Meets SAT Solving | by Jonas Poelmans | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@jonaspoelmans120/understanding-cp-sat-constraint-programming-meets-sat-solving-b137dddb943c\"},{\"title\":\"📈 #76 Optimizing with Google OR-Tools: strategies and insights from three years of real-world experience\",\"url\":\"https://www.feasible.club/p/76-optimizing-with-google-or-tools\"},{\"title\":\"ortools.sat.python.cp_model API documentation\",\"url\":\"https://or-tools.github.io/docs/pdoc/ortools/sat/python/cp_model.html\"},{\"title\":\"CP-SAT and incremental usage under assumptions: possible?\",\"url\":\"https://groups.google.com/g/or-tools-discuss/c/n8u8yoF4UOI\"},{\"title\":\"Incremental solving using CP-SAT solver · Issue #2014 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2014\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, I've found relevant information about CP-SAT, OR-Tools, incremental solving, and learned clauses:\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**CP-SAT Overview:**\nCP-SAT is a versatile portfolio solver centered around a Lazy Clause Generation (LCG) based Constraint Programming Solver, which translates CP constraints into a SAT formula (incrementally), and then uses a SAT solver to find a solution or prove unsatisfiability.\n\n**Learned Clauses:**\nThe key technique that makes SAT solving efficient is conflict-driven clause learning (CDCL). When the solver hits a dead end, it analyzes why the conflict occurred and learns a new clause preventing similar conflicts. Over the course of solving, the solver might learn thousands or millions of clauses. Restarts sound wasteful, but they're surprisingly effective because the solver retains all learned clauses.\n\n**Incremental Solving Limitations:**\nHowever, the search results indicate that incremental solving (adding constraints during callback) is a desired feature because the domain reductions and search tree of the previous search could be used as a starting point for a considerable decrease in runtime. This appears to be a feature request from 2020, suggesting it may not have been fully supported at that time.\n\n**Model Reuse:**\nRestarts rebuild the search tree from scratch, but keep learned clauses. This allows to recover from bad decisions, and lead to smaller search trees by learning from the mistakes of the past.\n\nThe search results suggest that while CP-SAT maintains learned clauses across restarts within a single solve, true incremental solving with model reuse across multiple solve calls appears to be a limitation or advanced feature of the solver.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"0d5d44a9-971c-45a5-b9e0-e217193def41","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:24.579Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bf7083a6-a050-4e11-a798-721fc36df9ea","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"19e8c517-a481-4330-9c8a-865a85d82ce3","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"ae3c21075cb388b14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ED1cBzmPwbpMxRDaNy13ye","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"SAT subsumption cache infeasibility certificate subset dominance pruning\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Certified Pruning from Counterfactual Consistency: Exact\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.07000\"},{\"title\":\"Certificate-Sensitive Subset Sum: Realizing Instance Complexity Jesus Salas∗\",\"url\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2507.15511v2\"},{\"title\":\"Handbook of Knowledge Representation 89\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.cornell.edu/gomes/pdf/2008_gomes_knowledge_satisfiability.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Certificate-Sensitive Subset Sum: Realizing Instance Complexity\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2507.15511v3\"},{\"title\":\"Certificate-Sensitive Subset Sum: Realizing Instance Complexity Jesus Salas∗\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2507.15511\"},{\"title\":\"Proof Complexity and SAT Solving\",\"url\":\"https://mathweb.ucsd.edu/~sbuss/ResearchWeb/ProofComplexitySAT/ProofComplexityChapter_June17.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Proof Complexity and SAT Solving\",\"url\":\"https://jakobnordstrom.se/docs/publications/ProofComplexityChapter.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"SAT-Based Subsumption Resolution | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-031-38499-8_11\"},{\"title\":\"SAT in Artiﬁcial Intelligence Introduction SAT NP-completeness\",\"url\":\"https://users.aalto.fi/~rintanj1/aaai14tutorialSAT/tutohandout-4up.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Computational Complexity: A Modern Approach - cs.Princeton\",\"url\":\"https://www.cs.princeton.edu/courses/archive/fall07/cos433/NPchap.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found regarding your query about SAT subsumption cache infeasibility certificate subset dominance pruning:\n\nThe search results contain relevant information about several of these concepts in the context of SAT solving:\n\n**Subsumption and Clause Learning:** The subsumption resolution rule lets one derive a clause A from (x ∨A) and (¬x ∨B) where B ⊆A (the derived clause A subsumes the antecedent (x ∨A)).\n\n**Infeasibility Certificates:** Certificates of infeasibility produced by the dual can be converted into learned clauses, allowing the solver to remember and reuse the global reasoning without repeating the linear program in subsequent branches.\n\n**Pruning Strategies:** Pairs (L, M) of clauses are pruned if they cannot be simplified by subsumption resolution due to a syntactic restriction, such as whether the set of predicates in L is a subset of the predicates in M.\n\n**Caching and Performance:** With caching and warm starts, many checks terminate quickly.\n\nThe search results primarily focus on subsumption resolution techniques in SAT-based theorem proving and CDCL (Conflict-Driven Clause Learning) SAT solvers, along with how infeasibility certificates can be leveraged for pruning the search space. If you're looking for more specific or recent research on these combined concepts, you may want to consult specialized SAT solver literature or proceedings from recent SAT solver competitions.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"20c02143-36dd-47a1-a1bd-797ba2823757","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:24.596Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"19e8c517-a481-4330-9c8a-865a85d82ce3","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
