{"parentUuid":"e2f376d7-95ea-4268-8b4c-a3d35155c41c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"ae3c21075cb388b14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01CD9kTsN6eQpUNzioTziFok","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"incremental SAT solver assumption-based interface MiniSAT Glucose persistent learned clauses\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Improving Glucose for Incremental SAT Solving with ...\",\"url\":\"http://www.cril.univ-artois.fr/articles/xmain.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - audemard/glucose: The glucose SAT solver · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/audemard/glucose\"},{\"title\":\"SAT Solving - The Next Generation Logic Framework\",\"url\":\"https://logicng.org/documentation/solvers/sat-solving/\"},{\"title\":\"Improving Glucose for Incremental SAT Solving with Assumptions: Application to MUS Extraction | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-642-39071-5_23\"},{\"title\":\"pysat/pysat/solvers.py at master · pysathq/pysat\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/pysathq/pysat/blob/master/pysat/solvers.py\"},{\"title\":\"SAT solvers’ API (pysat.solvers) — PySAT 1.9.dev2 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pysathq.github.io/docs/html/api/solvers.html\"},{\"title\":\"Speeding Up Assumption-Based SAT Randy Hickey(B) and Fahiem Bacchus(B)\",\"url\":\"http://www.cs.toronto.edu/~fbacchus/csc2512/Readings/Hickey-Bacchus2019_Chapter_SpeedingUpAssumption-BasedSAT.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Improving Glucose for Incremental SAT Solving with Assumptions: Application to MUS Extraction | Request PDF\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/262286933_Improving_Glucose_for_Incremental_SAT_Solving_with_Assumptions_Application_to_MUS_Extraction\"},{\"title\":\"CaDiCaL 2.0 Armin Biere1,2(B) , Tobias Faller1 , Katalin Fazekas3\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-031-65627-9_7.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Assessing Progress in SAT Solvers Through the Lens of Incremental SAT ⋆\",\"url\":\"https://alexeyignatiev.github.io/assets/pdf/kims-sat21-preprint.pdf\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found relevant information about incremental SAT solving with assumption-based interfaces in MiniSAT and Glucose solvers, along with techniques for managing persistent learned clauses. Here are the key findings:\n\n## Assumption-Based Interface\n\nIncrementality can be achieved with the use of the MiniSat-like assumption-based interface, which is helpful when multiple calls to a SAT solver are needed for the same formula using different sets of \"assumptions\".\n\n## Advantages and Challenges\n\nThere are several advantages of using assumptions: (1) it enables one to keep and reuse the clauses learnt during previous SAT calls at a later stage and (2) assumptions can be easily used to extract an unsatisfiable core of the formula. However, a drawback of assumption-based SAT solving is that the clauses learnt are longer (they typically contain many assumption literals).\n\n## Improving Glucose for Incremental SAT Solving\n\nThe work on refining the incremental SAT Solver Glucose from the SAT engine perspective addresses problems opened by this new use of SAT solvers, playing on clause database cleaning, assumptions managements and other classical parameters.\n\n## Persistent Learned Clauses with Selectors\n\nSince additional literals appear only positively in the formula, the learned clauses obtained during the search process keep track of all the initial clauses used to produce them. Then, removing the set of clauses which are derived from an initial clause with selector can be easily performed by simply assuming a to true.\n\nTo efficiently reuse learned clauses, it is essential to be able to distinguish which clauses may be useful for futures calls.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"274551c3-dcd2-461a-a011-549d0625278f","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:51.962Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e2f376d7-95ea-4268-8b4c-a3d35155c41c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"fa3adabf-6ed5-4a03-8bef-36cb010cef26","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"ae3c21075cb388b14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015UqGGLcWyyAfkw9HcBG8qk","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"CP-SAT or-tools assumption variables UNSAT core extraction\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"UnSAT core extraction with assumptions — CPMpy 0.9.23 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/linearize-power/unsat_core_extraction.html\"},{\"title\":\"Solving with assumptions — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/unsat_core_extraction.html\"},{\"title\":\"UnSAT core extraction with assumptions — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/complex_sudoku_examples/unsat_core_extraction.html\"},{\"title\":\"UnSAT core extraction with assumption variables — CPMpy 0.9.21 ...\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/stable/unsat_core_extraction.html\"},{\"title\":\"Parameters - The CP-SAT Primer - Using and Understanding Google OR-Tools' CP-SAT Solver\",\"url\":\"https://d-krupke.github.io/cpsat-primer/05_parameters.html\"},{\"title\":\"CPMpy ortools interface (cpmpy.solvers.ortools) — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/ufunc/api/solvers/ortools.html\"},{\"title\":\"Solvers — CPMpy 0.5 documentation - Read the Docs\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/latest/solvers.html\"},{\"title\":\"python cp-sat unsat assumption bug, is sat · Issue #2649 · google/or-tools\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/google/or-tools/issues/2649\"},{\"title\":\"Explanation tools (cpmpy.tools.explain) — CPMpy 0.9.24 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://cpmpy.readthedocs.io/en/simpl_bool_fix/api/tools/explain.html\"},{\"title\":\"Journal of Artiﬁcial Intelligence Research 40 (2011) 701-728\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1401.3878\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about CP-SAT and OR-Tools' assumption variables and UNSAT core extraction:\n\n## Overview\n\nOR-Tools is a Lazy Clause Generation solver built on SAT solvers and can inherit the ability to define assumptions and extract an unsatisfiable core. Since version 8.2, OR-Tools supports declaring assumptions and extracting an unsat core.\n\n## How It Works\n\nAssumptions can be used to explore different assignments of Boolean variables without reconstructing the model, and CP-SAT offers the extraction of unsatisfiable cores from infeasible models. In the satisfiability literature, the Boolean variables of interest are called assumption literals, and the solver will assume they are true. Any subset of these assumptions that, when true, makes the model unsatisfiable is called an unsatisfiable core.\n\n## Practical Usage\n\nWhen the solver returns UNSAT, get_core() returns a small subset of assumption variables that are unsat together. CPMpy will return only those variables that are False in the UNSAT core.\n\nThe method `solver.sufficient_assumptions_for_infeasibility()` can be used to retrieve the minimal unsat core.\n\n## Applications\n\nThis functionality opens the door to more advanced use cases, such as Minimal Unsatisfiable Subsets (MUS) and QuickXplain-like tools to help debugging.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"f15305a3-690c-4174-9c0e-fc60add8c766","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:52.444Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fa3adabf-6ed5-4a03-8bef-36cb010cef26","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"08934cf7-db18-4b9d-931c-7de7aca47227","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"fa4d4bc6-e2b2-445f-8cd0-cc97f0106215","agentId":"ae3c21075cb388b14","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017izpJE4uBeN7uDJZRFTT1g","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"vehicle routing\" subproblem caching column generation memoization speedup\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"A Column-Generation-Based Exact Algorithm to Solve the Full-Truckload Vehicle-Routing Problem\",\"url\":\"https://www.mdpi.com/2227-7390/13/5/876\"},{\"title\":\"Accelerating Strategies in Column Generation Methods for Vehicle Routing and Crew Scheduling Problems | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-1-4615-1507-4_14\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - Pigzaum/cg_cvrp: A column generation algorithm (CG) for the capacitated vehicle routing problem (CVRP) · GitHub\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/Pigzaum/cg_cvrp\"},{\"title\":\"Improving Column-Generation for Vehicle Routing Problems via\",\"url\":\"https://optimization-online.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/03/8292.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Solving Single Depot Capacitated Vehicle Routing Problem Using Column Generation with Python - OR & Data Science Stories\",\"url\":\"https://emrahcimren.github.io/operations%20research/Solving-Single-Depot-Capacitated-Vehicle-Routing-Problem-Using-Column-Generation-with-Python/\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - ec0di/pyVrp: Solves the Vehicle Routing Problem (VRP) using Column Generation (CG). It is made as an inspiration to use CG in more projects, since it can be quite effective in the right situations.\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/ec0di/pyVrp\"},{\"title\":\"How to Implement Column Generation for Vehicle Routing | by Shannon Kelley | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@shannon-optimizes/how-to-implement-column-generation-for-vehicle-routing-bdb8027c957f\"},{\"title\":\"1 A Column Generation Algorithm for Vehicle Scheduling and Routing Problems\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1806.00831\"},{\"title\":\"A Column Generation Algorithm for a Rich Vehicle-Routing Problem | Transportation Science\",\"url\":\"https://pubsonline.informs.org/doi/10.1287/trsc.1080.0256\"},{\"title\":\"Column generation for vehicle routing problems with multiple synchronization constraints - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0377221718305988\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what I found about vehicle routing, subproblems, and acceleration techniques in column generation:\n\n## Key Findings\n\n**Column Generation for Vehicle Routing:**\nColumn generation is used with accelerating strategies to solve vehicle routing and crew scheduling problems, with techniques directed at speeding up each of the five phases: pre-processor, subproblem, master problem, branch-and-bound, and post-optimizer.\n\n**Subproblem Acceleration:**\nA label-setting algorithm is used to generate columns within the exact algorithm by being accelerated through bi-directional search, ng-route relaxation, subproblem selection, and heuristic column generation. The same acceleration techniques apply across different vehicle routing implementations.\n\n**Parallel Computing Speedup:**\nThe random coloring algorithm gets significantly boosted with parallel computing because runs for different color-codings are completely independent, with speedup factors ranging from 5 to 14 using two 20-core processors.\n\n**Caching and Solution Strategy:**\nIn each iteration of column generation, a vector is maintained corresponding to subset-row cuts with non-zero dual variables, with counters for each subset-row cut that are updated when labels extend to nodes in subsets.\n\nIn practical applications, these acceleration methods often were key elements for the viability of the optimization approach, with computational gains enabling solutions that could not have been obtained otherwise due to practical problem complexity and size.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d270839c-2e27-4592-ae25-a2409653cfe2","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:41:52.620Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"08934cf7-db18-4b9d-931c-7de7aca47227","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
